+-    "content": "# [Informational] Studio Configuration Reference\n\nAuthoritative technical reference specification for all configuration attributes, execution tiers, and administrative controls across all 14 \ud83d\udc4dkanban Studios.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Workspace Studio\nThe Workspace Studio (`/workspaces/:ws/settings` or `/admin/workspaces`) manages top-level tenancy, membership defaults, data lifecycle policies, and security guardrails.\n\n\n### Workspace Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `name` | Human-readable name of the workspace displayed in header and navigation | UTF-8 String (1 to 128 characters) | Required at provisioning | Public within workspace; visible to all members and invited guests |\n| `slug` | URL-safe workspace identifier used in API routes and web paths | Lowercase alphanumeric ASCII with hyphens (2 to 64 chars) | Derived from name | Immutable after creation; binds database RLS tenancy context |\n| `default_role` | Default permission tier assigned to newly provisioned local users | `WORKSPACE_MEMBER`, `WORKSPACE_VIEWER`, `WORKSPACE_GUEST` | `WORKSPACE_MEMBER` | Restricts baseline capabilities before explicit project assignments |\n| `trash_retention_days` | Number of days soft-deleted items remain in Trash Studio before automated purge | Integer between 7 and 365 | 30 | Governs compliance data retention; automated purge requires daemon runner |\n| `auto_archive_threshold_days` | Inactivity duration after which closed tickets are automatically archived | Integer between 0 and 730 (0 disables auto-archive) | 90 | Keeps active board queries lightweight; archived tickets remain search-indexable |\n| `max_projects_limit` | Maximum number of active projects allowed within this workspace | Positive integer (1 to 1000) | 50 | Resource cap preventing tenant sprawl and memory exhaustion |\n| `air_gap_enforced` | Strict zero-egress flag blocking all external cloud AI and runner connections | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `false` | When enabled, only local Ollama models and internal runners execute |\n| `allowed_email_domains` | Comma-separated domain allowlist for user registration and invite cards | List of valid domain strings (e.g. `corp.example.com`) | Empty (Allow all verified) | Rejects non-corporate email addresses at registration gate |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Project Studio\nThe Project Studio (`/projects/:proj/settings`) configures project-level attributes, work item defaults, view presets, and planning dimensions.\n\n\n### Project Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `name` | Display name of the project | UTF-8 String (1 to 128 characters) | Required at creation | Scoped to workspace; surfaced in command palette and breadcrumbs |\n| `key` | Prefix key for issue identifiers (e.g. `KAN-142`) | Uppercase ASCII alphanumeric (2 to 10 characters) | Required at creation | Immutable; generates human-readable issue keys across all surfaces |\n| `description` | Markdown overview and charter for the project | Canonical UTF-8 Markdown (up to 10,000 characters) | Empty | Sanitized server-side; safe Mermaid and media proxying enabled |\n| `default_workflow_id` | Identifier of active workflow managing state transitions | Valid UUID of existing project workflow | Default Kanban Workflow | Determines active transition gates, validators, and AI agent checks |\n| `auto_archive_days` | Project-specific threshold for archiving closed work items | Integer (0 to 365, 0 = inherit workspace) | 0 | Overrides workspace retention for high-velocity software teams |\n| `enable_cycles` | Toggles Agile/Scrum cycle and sprint planning features | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `true` | Enables cycle velocity tracking, start/end dates, and rollover |\n| `enable_modules` | Toggles functional module breakdown and progress roll-ups | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `true` | Allows hierarchical grouping of work items under functional deliverables |\n| `wip_limits_enforcement` | Behavior when Kanban column WIP limits are exceeded | `warn`, `strict_block`, `disabled` | `warn` | `strict_block` rejects card drop operations exceeding column capacity |\n| `default_assignee_mode` | Initial assignment policy for newly authored work items | `unassigned`, `project_lead`, `creator` | `unassigned` | Prevents orphaned tickets and automates triage assignment |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Workflow Transition Studio\nThe Workflow Transition Studio (`/projects/:proj/settings/workflows`) defines state groups, transition helper hooks, synchronous validators, and asynchronous AI agent gates.\n\n\n### Workflow Transition Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `state_name` | Human-readable label for the workflow state | String (1 to 64 characters) | Required (e.g. `In Review`) | Appears on board columns, peek drawers, and transition selectors |\n| `state_group` | Standardized category mapping for cycle metrics | `backlog`, `unstarted`, `started`, `completed`, `cancelled` | `started` | Used in velocity burn-down and sub-issue progress roll-ups |\n| `helper_type` | Engine used to evaluate transition eligibility | `wasm_validator`, `wasm_mutator`, `agent_gate` | `wasm_validator` | Governs execution latency and synchronous vs asynchronous processing |\n| `hook_path` | Target module, wasm bytecode, or agent identifier | File path, slug, or registered agent UUID | Required | Executes within sandboxed environment with strict resource caps |\n| `failure_policy` | Action taken if transition helper rejects or times out | `BLOCK_TRANSITION`, `WARN_AND_ALLOW`, `PROVISIONAL_HOLD` | `BLOCK_TRANSITION` | Fail-closed invariant preventing invalid transitions into protected states |\n| `execution_timeout_ms` | Maximum allowed wall-clock execution time | 50ms to 30000ms (Sync Wasm: <=250ms) | 100ms | Prevents hanging transitions and thread pool starvation |\n| `sandbox_tier` | Isolation level enforced during helper execution | `wasm` (in-process wasmtime) or `nsjail` (container) | `wasm` | Sandboxed with zero network or filesystem syscall access |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Custom Fields Studio\nThe Custom Fields Studio (`/projects/:proj/settings/custom-fields`) defines dynamic metadata, computed story points, and sandboxed formulas.\n\n\n### Custom Fields Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `name` | Human-readable label displayed on ticket rails and tables | String (1 to 64 characters) | Required | Displayed alongside execution status badge (`\ud83e\uddea`) |\n| `slug` | Unique field identifier used in formulas and JSON payloads | Lowercase alphanumeric ASCII with hyphens | Derived from name | Used in formula expressions and API JSON properties |\n| `flavor` | Execution tier and computation engine | `scalar`, `formula`, `wasm`, `nsjail` | `formula` | Determines performance budget and standalone mode availability |\n| `sandbox_tier` | Multi-tier sandbox engine used for execution | `scalar`, `wasmtime` (Tier A), `nsjail` (Tier B) | `wasmtime` | `nsjail` requires multi-container compose; fail-closed in standalone |\n| `credential_mode` | Token identity provided during script execution | `INVOKING_USER`, `SERVICE_ACCOUNT` | `INVOKING_USER` | `SERVICE_ACCOUNT` requires dual-control cryptographic approval |\n| `target_scope` | Maximum tenant visibility boundary of the field | `PROJECT`, `WORKSPACE`, `SERVER` | `PROJECT` | Escalation beyond `PROJECT` requires AAL2 administrative step-up |\n| `output_format` | Canonical schema used to format and render output | `text`, `number`, `badge`, `severity_pill`, `url`, `markdown_snippet`, `json_table` | `text` | Enforces strict Pydantic validation before persisting to JSONB |\n| `format_metadata` | Configuration options tailored to chosen output format | JSON object matching schema specification | `{}` | Governs decimal precision, color tokens, allowlists, or table headers |\n| `script_source` | Raw source code for formula or script evaluation | Python AST expression, Rust Wasm, or container script | Required | Signed with SHA-256 digest upon commit; immutable against database tampering |\n| `egress_allowlist` | Allowed destination hostnames for nsjail tier | Comma-separated domain list (e.g. `api.github.com`) | Empty (Zero egress) | Network disabled by default via Linux kernel `CLONE_NEWNET` |\n\n---\n\n## 5. Authentication Studio\nThe Multi-Card Authentication Studio (`/admin/auth`) configures enterprise identity providers, WebAuthn Passkeys, LDAP trees, and break-glass recovery.\n\n\n### Authentication Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `provider_type` | Underlying identity protocol card | `LOCAL_ADMIN`, `OAUTH2`, `OIDC`, `LDAP`, `WEBAUTHN`, `TOTP` | Required | Determines credential validation handshake and token exchange |\n| `card_enabled` | Master active toggle for the authentication card | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `true` | Disabled cards reject authentication attempts immediately |\n| `sort_rank` | Order in which login cards appear on `/login` screen | Non-negative integer (lower ranks appear first) | 10 | Allows organizations to prioritize SSO while preserving local admin cards |\n| `rp_name` | WebAuthn Relying Party display name | String (Always canonical `\ud83d\udc4dkanban`) | `\ud83d\udc4dkanban` | Displayed by browser and hardware security keys during passkey prompts |\n| `rp_id` | WebAuthn Relying Party domain identifier | Strict ASCII FQDN without protocol or port | Hostname of deployment | Must match browser origin to prevent credential theft or spoofing |\n| `issuer_url` | OIDC discovery endpoint URL | Valid HTTPS URL | None | Fetches `/.well-known/openid-configuration` and cryptographic keys |\n| `client_id` | OAuth/OIDC application client identifier | ASCII string | None | Public identifier registered with enterprise identity provider |\n| `client_secret` | OAuth/OIDC client secret credential | Encrypted string (write-only custody) | None | Stored encrypted in PostgreSQL; never exposed over REST API |\n| `ldap_host` | LDAPS directory hostname or IP address | Hostname or IP | None | LDAPS on port 636 or StartTLS required; plaintext LDAP rejected |\n| `ldap_bind_dn` | Distinguished Name used for directory lookup | LDAP DN string (e.g. `cn=admin,dc=corp,dc=com`) | None | Service account with read-only search permissions across user tree |\n| `break_glass_active` | Emergency lockout prevention bypass trigger | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `false` | Allows emergency local admin access when SSO is offline |\n\n---\n\n## 6. MCP Access Studio\nThe MCP Access Studio (`/settings/mcp` or `/admin/mcp`) manages Model Context Protocol API tokens, Level of Detail (LOD) defaults, and rate limits.\n\n\n### MCP Access Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `surface_type` | Target isolated streamable endpoint | `/mcp/agent`, `/mcp/docs`, `/mcp/admin` | `/mcp/agent` | Determines tool catalog and token budget ceiling (<= 1,850 tokens for agent) |\n| `key_name` | Descriptive name of the API token or client tool | String (1 to 64 characters) | Required | Surfaced in audit logs and active session inspector |\n| `token_prefix` | Standardized key prefix identifying privilege level | `kb_agt_*`, `kb_doc_*`, `kb_adm_*` | Auto-assigned | Cryptographically identifies target surface before database lookup |\n| `lod_tier` | Default Level of Detail returned in tool responses | `summary`, `standard`, `full` | `standard` | Controls response verbosity; `summary` minimizes agent context consumption |\n| `rate_limit_rpm` | Maximum allowed tool invocations per minute | Integer between 10 and 600 | 120 | Protects database connection pool from runaway autonomous agent loops |\n| `expires_at` | Expiration timestamp for the API key | ISO-8601 Timestamp or `None` | 90 days from creation | Expired keys reject calls with `401 UNAUTHORIZED` |\n| `destructive_gate` | Enables two-stage confirmation for destructive operations | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `true` | Requires short-lived confirmation token before permanent deletions |\n| `capability_mask` | Operational capability bitmask ceiling | Integer bitmask (1=View, 3=Comment, 31=Member, 255=Admin) | 255 (Full) | Monotonic `CredentialBoundary` ceiling; mask 1 restricts scopes to `${surface}:read` |\n| `allowed_cidrs` | Subnet IP allowlist for incoming MCP invocations | Comma or whitespace separated IPv4/IPv6 CIDRs | None (Allow all) | Rejects calls originating outside specified subnets with `403 FORBIDDEN_CIDR` |\n\n---\n\n## 7. OAuth 2.1 Governance Studio\nThe OAuth 2.1 Governance Studio (`/admin/oauth`) registers client applications, enforces PKCE code challenges, and configures token lifetimes.\n\n\n### OAuth 2.1 Governance Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `client_name` | Name of the integrating client application | String (1 to 128 characters) | Required | Displayed on user authorization consent screens |\n| `client_id` | Unique OAuth client identifier | String prefixed with `kb_client_` | Auto-generated | Public application key used during authorization code flow |\n| `redirect_uris` | Allowed callback URLs for authorization codes | List of strict HTTPS URLs (localhost allowed for dev) | Required | Exact string matching enforced; wildcards strictly forbidden |\n| `pkce_enforced` | Proof Key for Code Exchange requirement | Mandatory `S256` challenge | `S256` | Rejects plaintext code challenges; mitigates authorization code interception |\n| `allowed_scopes` | List of privilege scopes client can request | `agent`, `docs`, `admin`, `read:issues`, `write:issues` | `agent` | Token capability bitmask cannot exceed granted scopes |\n| `access_token_ttl` | Lifetime of minted access tokens | Integer seconds (300 to 86400) | 3600 (1 hour) | Ephemeral lifetime limiting blast radius of token leakage |\n| `refresh_token_ttl` | Lifetime of rotating refresh tokens | Integer seconds (86400 to 2592000) | 2592000 (30 days) | Refresh tokens rotate upon use; reused tokens revoke family |\n\n---\n\n## 8. AI Providers Studio\nThe AI Providers Studio (`/admin/ai`) registers model providers, configures write-only API keys, and sets monthly token consumption caps.\n\n\n### AI Providers Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `provider_key` | Underlying AI provider implementation | `openai`, `gemini`, `ollama`, `custom` | `gemini` | Selects prompt serializer, tool calling dialect, and streaming pipeline |\n| `priority` | Fallback dispatch order when multiple providers are active | Integer (1 to 100, higher runs first) | 10 | Primary provider attempted first; falls back on rate limit or 5xx |\n| `api_endpoint` | Base URL for API communications | Valid HTTP/HTTPS URL | Official cloud endpoint | Local Ollama uses `http://localhost:11434`; cloud requires HTTPS |\n| `api_key` | Cryptographic API secret for provider access | String (Write-only custody) | None | Stored encrypted in PostgreSQL; never sent back to browser client |\n| `monthly_token_cap` | Monthly token consumption budget ceiling | Non-negative integer (0 = unlimited) | 1,000,000 | Blocks cloud invocations once budget is reached to prevent cost overruns |\n| `default_model` | Model identifier used for sparkle and transition gates | String (e.g. `gemini-2.5-flash`, `gpt-4o`) | Provider default | Balances reasoning capability against response latency |\n| `temperature` | Randomness parameter for model generations | Float between 0.0 and 1.0 | 0.2 | Low temperatures ensure deterministic responses for code and QA gates |\n\n---\n\n## 9. Agent Definitions Studio\nThe Agent Definitions Studio (`/admin/agents`) authors system personas, binds autonomous agents to service accounts, and configures execution harnesses.\n\n\n### Agent Definitions Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `agent_name` | Unique name and identifier for the autonomous agent | String (1 to 64 characters) | Required | Used in ticket mentions, activity streams, and assignment cards |\n| `system_prompt` | Core instructions, persona guidelines, and security boundaries | UTF-8 Markdown text | Required | Directs model reasoning; stripped of dangerous injection sequences |\n| `execution_harness` | Runtime environment executing agent invocations | `api_direct`, `cli_pipe`, `remote_runner` | `api_direct` | `cli_pipe` uses zero-shell `agy -p` or `codex -p` in Linux jail |\n| `cli_pipe_binary` | Binary path used for zero-shell CLI pipe harness | Pre-approved binary name (`agy`, `codex`) | `agy` | Executes without shell or bash; communicates strictly over STDIN/STDOUT |\n| `service_principal_id` | Bound service account identity for database RLS | Valid UUID of service principal | Auto-provisioned | Binds database permissions to caller identity via `SET LOCAL` |\n| `repository_access` | Git repository access mode granted to the agent | `none`, `ro_mount`, `ephemeral_clone` | `none` | `ro_mount` mounts repository strictly read-only for forensic QA |\n\n---\n\n## 10. Remote Runners Studio\nThe Remote Runners Studio (`/admin/agent-runners`) configures distributed build nodes, mints enrollment tokens, and monitors mTLS heartbeats over dedicated port `:8444`.\n\n\n### Remote Runners Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `runner_name` | Unique hostname or identifier of the runner node | String (1 to 64 characters) | Required | Displayed in runner health dashboard and active job telemetry |\n| `enrollment_token` | Single-use cryptographic token for runner registration | Base64-encoded HMAC string | Minted on demand | Expires after 15 minutes (900 seconds max); grants certificate signing request (CSR) |\n| `mtls_cert_fingerprint`| SHA-256 fingerprint of validated client certificate | Hexadecimal SHA-256 hash | Registered upon enroll | Hardware-bound identity verifying mutual TLS WebSocket connection over port :8444 |\n| `runner_cli_command` | Standard enrollment invocation command | Shell command string | Generated on mint | Syntax: `echo \"$TOKEN\" \\| uv run kanban-agent-runner enroll --server-url <origin> --token-stdin --pool <pool>` |\n| `max_concurrent_jobs` | Maximum simultaneous task executions allowed on runner | Integer between 1 and 16 | 2 | Throttles runner workload to prevent resource exhaustion |\n| `heartbeat_timeout_s` | Maximum allowable duration without ping before offline | Integer between 10 and 120 seconds | 30 | Dead runners are marked offline and active jobs rescheduled |\n| `allowed_repo_remotes` | Whitelist of Git repository URLs runner can clone | Comma-separated list of repository URLs | Empty (All authorized) | Prevents runner exploitation for arbitrary network exfiltration |\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Runner Certificate Rotation Lifecycle:** Runner client certificates are hardware-bound and cannot be silently renewed. To rotate a certificate, revoke the runner in `/admin/agent-runners` (AAL2 challenge confirmed), mint a fresh enrollment token (15-minute TTL), and re-enroll the runner daemon.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Standalone & Promotion Studio\nThe Standalone & Promotion Studio (`/admin/standalone`) inspects deployment topologies, triggers promotion to multi-container compose, and exports encrypted backups.\n\n![Standalone Status Studio - Desktop Light](images/slice4-standalone-1440x900-light.png)\n\n\n### Standalone & Promotion Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `deployment_mode` | Current platform runtime environment | `standalone_embedded_pg`, `production_multi_container` | Detected at boot | Gates availability of nsjail container sandboxes and Redis hubs |\n| `passphrase` | Secret passphrase used to derive AES-256 backup key | String (Minimum 16 characters) | User-supplied | Encrypts archive into streaming KBKP or snapshot KBBAK1 format |\n| `auto_backup_enabled` | Recurring automated background snapshot toggle | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `false` | Instructs .kb_ops.sock daemon to create automated snapshots |\n| `backup_interval_hours`| Frequency of automated snapshot execution | Integer between 1 and 168 hours | `24` | Governs Recovery Point Objective (RPO) and background IO scheduling |\n| `snapshot_retention_count` | Number of historical snapshot archives retained on server disk | Integer between 1 and 90 | `7` | Purges older snapshots automatically while keeping latest N snapshots |\n| `confirm_overwrite` | Confirmation phrase required for destructive restore | Exact string `\"RESTORE\"` | None | Prevents accidental database erasure during snapshot restoration |\n| `backup_storage_path` | Local filesystem path where backups are stored | Directory path (`/var/lib/kanban/data/backups`) | `/var/lib/kanban/data/backups` | Isolated storage managed by `/run/postgresql/.kb_ops.sock` daemon |\n| `compose_networks` | Network isolation topology for exported compose bundle | 5 networks (`edge_net`, `app_net`, `data_net`, `sandbox_net`, `egress_llm_net`) | 5 networks | Eliminates single-network lateral movement across services |\n\n![Standalone Backups and Snapshot History](images/slice4-standalone-backups-snapshot-history.png)\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs\n\n- **`deployment_mode`:** Detects whether \ud83d\udc4dkanban is executing inside a single standalone container (`standalone_embedded_pg`, typically launched via `docker run -p 8080:8080 thumbs-up-kanban:latest` with internal PostgreSQL 16) or within a compartmentalized production cluster (`production_multi_container`). In standalone mode, external Redis is optional and background tasks run in-process, whereas multi-container mode unlocks dedicated worker containers and nsjail container isolation.\n- **`passphrase`:** The client-supplied cryptographic passphrase submitted during manual streaming backup download (`POST /api/v1/admin/standalone/backup`) and database restoration (`POST /api/v1/admin/standalone/restore`). Must be at least 16 characters in length. The server derives an AES-256-GCM symmetric key via PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 across 600,000 iterations for KBKP streaming downloads, or Argon2id for KBBAK1 archives.\n- **`auto_backup_enabled`:** Toggles automated recurring background snapshots executed by the root-privileged `/run/postgresql/.kb_ops.sock` daemon. When enabled, the daemon captures full PostgreSQL state and object graph assets into `/var/lib/kanban/data/backups` using the timestamped `KBBAK1` format without interrupting active HTTP or WebSocket connections.\n- **`backup_interval_hours`:** Configures the interval between automated snapshot runs. The backend permits values between 1 and 168 hours (default 24 hours). This setting determines how frequently the `/run/postgresql/.kb_ops.sock` daemon wakes to execute scheduled pg_dump snapshot routines.\n- **`snapshot_retention_count`:** Defines the maximum number of automated snapshot `.kbbak` archives preserved within `/var/lib/kanban/data/backups` (configurable from 1 to 90, default 7). When a newly generated snapshot exceeds this threshold, the snapshot manager unlinks the oldest archives while verifying that at least the configured retention count remains accessible.\n- **`confirm_overwrite`:** The mandatory safety gate required by the destructive database restore endpoint (`POST /api/v1/admin/standalone/restore`). The payload must contain the exact uppercase string `\"RESTORE\"`. Any request containing mismatched, lowercase, or omitted confirmation tokens is immediately rejected with HTTP 422 before any database sequence is touched or tables are dropped.\n- **`backup_storage_path`:** The server-side directory where automated `.kbbak` archives reside (`/var/lib/kanban/data/backups`). This path is protected with restrictive permissions and managed exclusively by the fixed-operation socket daemon.\n- **`compose_networks`:** The 5-network topology exported by `GET /api/v1/admin/standalone/compose-export`. Separates traffic across `edge_net` (Caddy TLS ingress), `app_net` (FastAPI backend), `data_net` (PostgreSQL 16 and Redis), `sandbox_net` (untrusted Wasm and runner execution), and `egress_llm_net` (outbound AI provider traffic). Accompanied by a secret-scrubbed `.env.example` file.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Migration Import Studio\nThe Migration Import Studio (`/admin/imports`) provides lossless data ingestion from Plane, Jira, GitHub Issues, Linear, and canonical portable workspace bundles.\n\n![Migration Import Studio Multi-Source Tabs](images/slice4-imports-multi-source-tabs.png)\n\n\n### Migration Import Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `workspace_slug` | Destination workspace tenant identifier | Lowercase alphanumeric ASCII with hyphens | Active workspace | Attaches imported entities to specified tenant under PostgreSQL RLS |\n| `project_key` | Destination project prefix key (e.g. `KAN`, `JIRA`) | Uppercase ASCII alphanumeric (2 to 10 chars) | Bundle default | Re-keys issues sequentially while preserving historical keys as aliases |\n| `dry_run` | Non-destructive simulation toggle | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `false` | Calculates entity diffs inside a database savepoint without persisting |\n| `user_map` | Mapping dictionary from source users to canonical users | JSON dictionary of strings | `{}` | Binds historical authors, assignees, and commenters to active principals |\n| `export_workspace_slug` | Target workspace for generating portable bundles | Existing workspace slug | Current | Exports canonical `.tar.gz` archive containing entities, docs, and links |\n| `export_scrub` | Redacts sensitive secrets and tokens during export | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `false` | Scrubs passwords, bearer tokens, and credentials from markdown before export |\n\n![Migration Import Studio - Desktop Light](images/slice4-imports-1440x900-light.png)\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs\n\n- **`per_source_endpoints`:** Ingestion is executed via 4 distinct per-platform endpoints: `POST /api/v1/admin/imports/plane` (Plane workspace JSON), `POST /api/v1/admin/imports/jira` (Jira Cloud/Server JSON or REST dump with ADF support), `POST /api/v1/admin/imports/github` (GitHub Issues REST/CLI JSON), and `POST /api/v1/admin/imports/linear` (Linear JSON archive). Each parser normalizes rich text into sanitized UTF-8 CommonMark while strictly rejecting Unicode lone surrogates.\n- **`workspace_slug` and `project_key`:** Defines the destination tenant context and issue key prefix for the imported dataset. If specified, issues are allocated sequential integer sequence IDs under the destination project key (e.g. `KAN-1`, `KAN-2`). If left empty, the importer provisions or resolves the workspace and project names specified within the input bundle.\n- **`dry_run`:** Controls whether database modifications are committed. When set to `true`, the importer runs within a PostgreSQL `SAVEPOINT`, parses all entities, validates foreign keys, checks user mappings, and emits an `ImportDiffReport` before rolling back the savepoint. When set to `false`, the operation executes within an atomic database transaction that commits only upon 100% validation success.\n- **`user_map`:** A JSON dictionary mapping source account identifiers, legacy usernames, or email addresses to canonical \ud83d\udc4dkanban user accounts (e.g. `{\"legacy_dev\": \"alice@example.com\"}`). Unmapped accounts fall back to system defaults, ensuring that ownership, assignments, and historical comments remain traceable.\n- **`legacy_key_aliases`:** Upstream issue keys (such as `JIRA-101`, `GH-88`, `ENG-42`) are automatically extracted and recorded in the `issue_key_aliases` catalog. When queries arrive for obsolete keys (`GET /api/v1/issues/{issue_key}`), API clients receive `200 OK` JSON with header `X-Kanban-Canonical-Key: {canonical_key}`, while browser requests requesting `text/html` receive `HTTP 308 Permanent Redirect` with `Location: /projects/{project_key}/issues/{canonical_key}`.\n- **`export_workspace_slug` and `export_scrub`:** Used by the portable workspace exporter (`POST /api/v1/admin/exports/portable`) to generate a compressed `.tar.gz` bundle. When `scrub=true` (`export_scrub` in UI), secret patterns and authentication tokens are actively redacted from prose fields before packaging.\n\n---\n\n## 13. Admin Diagnostics & Realtime Probes Studio\nThe Admin Diagnostics Studio (`/admin/diagnostics`) monitors subsystem health, executes active telemetry probes, and tracks container resource limits.\n\n![Admin Diagnostics Studio - Desktop Light](images/slice4-diagnostics-1440x900-light.png)\n\n\n### Admin Diagnostics Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `postgresql_rls` | PostgreSQL connectivity, pool saturation, migrations, RLS | 82 migrations, 100 tables `FORCE RLS` | Continuous | Core persistence and tenant isolation; latency threshold < 15ms |\n| `realtime_hub_outbox` | Transactional outbox relay and dispatch loop | Backlog depth, lag seconds, listener status | Continuous | Event delivery to WebSocket subscribers; lag threshold < 5s |\n| `job_worker_pool` | Background asynchronous task execution engine | Active tasks, worker concurrency, DLQ depth | Continuous | Processes asynchronous jobs; dead-letter queue must equal 0 |\n| `wasm_script_sandbox` | Wasmtime AST compiler and script sandbox execution | Compiler state, memory limits, fuel budget | Continuous | Sandboxed custom fields; fuel caps prevent infinite execution loops |\n| `agent_runtime_providers`| Health and latency of active AI model providers | `openai`, `gemini`, `ollama` provider status | Continuous | Informs sparkle completions and AI gates; reports monthly budget |\n| `remote_runner_fleet` | Connected mTLS build runner nodes over port :8444 | Online runner count, heartbeat timestamp | Continuous | Distributed workload execution; heartbeats must arrive within 30s |\n| `rate_limiter` | In-memory token bucket and IP throttle health | Bucket capacity, blocked IP count, error rate | Continuous | Protects API endpoints from volumetric denial-of-service |\n| `realtime_pubsub_probe` | Active PostgreSQL LISTEN/NOTIFY latency measurement | Latency milliseconds (target < 1.0ms) | On Demand | Validates database pub/sub bus responsiveness |\n| `websocket_ws_probe` | Real WebSocket ping/pong frame roundtrip measurement | Latency milliseconds (target < 50ms) | On Demand | Validates client-to-server WebSocket bidirectional messaging |\n\n![Admin Diagnostics Realtime Probe Active](images/slice4-diagnostics-realtime-probe-active.png)\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs\n\n- **`postgresql_rls`:** Evaluates database health via `GET /api/v1/admin/diagnostics`. Asserts that the single-head Alembic migration tree is fully applied (82/82 migrations), connection pool saturation remains under 75%, and all 100 application tables enforce `FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY`. Connection latency is sampled continuously with healthy thresholds < 15ms.\n- **`realtime_hub_outbox`:** Tracks the transactional outbox relay daemon (`database/outbox/relay.py`). Measures the unprocessed message queue depth, average dispatch lag (healthy < 5s), and PostgreSQL `LISTEN/NOTIFY` channel subscription status. Stalled outboxes trigger immediate administrative alerts.\n- **`job_worker_pool`:** Monitors asynchronous background task executors responsible for webhook dispatch, automated snapshot creation, and audit logging. Reports total active tasks, concurrency utilization, and dead-letter queue (DLQ) depth. Any non-zero DLQ count indicates unhandled job failures requiring inspection.\n- **`wasm_script_sandbox`:** Monitors the in-process Wasmtime runtime executing custom field formulas and transition helpers. Verifies compiler initialization, memory consumption caps (default 32 MiB / 33,554,432 bytes per instance), and fuel budget limits preventing CPU exhaustion.\n- **`agent_runtime_providers`:** Asserts the availability and response latency of registered AI model providers (`gemini`, `openai`, `ollama`). Reports monthly token consumption against configured budget ceilings, warning administrators before cloud budget caps halt automated transition gates.\n- **`remote_runner_fleet`:** Tracks external execution nodes connected via mutual TLS (mTLS) over dedicated port `:8444`. Reports active runner counts, certificate expiration dates, and heartbeat timestamps. Runners failing to report within 30 seconds are marked offline and their active leases rescheduled.\n- **`rate_limiter`:** Reports the runtime state of in-memory token bucket rate limiters across public and authenticated routes. Tracks blocked request volumes and active IP throttle mappings, ensuring system resilience against abusive traffic.\n- **`realtime_pubsub_probe` and `websocket_ws_probe`:** On-demand latency probes. The pub/sub probe (`POST /api/v1/admin/diagnostics/probes/realtime`) injects a synthetic outbox notification and captures `LISTEN/NOTIFY` roundtrip time (typically ~0.38ms). The WebSocket probe initiates a live `/ws` binary ping frame in the operator's browser, measuring connection setup (~13.7ms) and ping/pong roundtrip latency (~0.44ms).\n\n---\n\n## 14. Universal Object Graph Link Auditor Studio\nThe Universal Object Graph Link Auditor Studio (`/admin/link-auditor`) inspects, classifies, and remediates referential integrity anomalies across all registered entities.\n\n![Link Auditor Studio - Desktop Light](images/slice4-link-auditor-1440x900-light.png)\n\n\n### Link Auditor Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `audit_ids` | Flagged link audit record UUIDs selected for purge | List of 1 to 200 UUIDs | Mandatory in purge | Scope of destructive link deletion in single atomic transaction |\n| `reason` | Mandatory administrative justification for link purge | String (10 to 500 characters) | Mandatory in purge | Recorded in immutable activity ledger; rejects < 10 chars with 422 |\n| `status_filter` | Filter tabs for inspecting audit record lifecycle | `unpurged`, `purged`, `resolved`, `all` | `unpurged` | Isolates active anomalies from resolved historical records |\n| `reason_filter` | Filters links by formal invalidity category | All 7 `InvalidLinkReason` codes | `ALL` | Isolates specific defect patterns for forensic inspection |\n\n![Link Auditor Integrity Scan Active](images/slice4-link-auditor-integrity-scan-active.png)\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs\n\n- **`audit_scan`:** Triggered via `POST /api/v1/admin/object-links:audit` (or the **Run Integrity Scan** button). Traverses `object_links` and returns summary counts across all 7 formal `InvalidLinkReason` codes without acquiring table locks.\n- **`reason_filter`:** Allows administrators to filter detected anomalies across the 7 formal `InvalidLinkReason` classes:\n  1. `DANGLING_SOURCE`: Source object UUID does not exist in the universal object spine.\n  2. `DANGLING_TARGET`: Target object UUID does not exist in the database.\n  3. `SOFT_DELETED_ENDPOINT`: One or both endpoints reference an object soft-deleted beyond the retention window.\n  4. `ARCHIVED_POLICY_VIOLATION`: Link connects active and archived entities violating project archiving rules.\n  5. `TYPE_MATRIX_VIOLATION`: Relation violates the registered link type matrix allowlist.\n  6. `CROSS_WORKSPACE_LEAK`: Link connects entities across disjoint workspace boundaries without federation.\n  7. `PERMISSION_SEVERED`: Endpoints span boundaries where caller or entity permissions have been severed.\n- **`audit_ids`:** The explicit list of 1 to 200 audit record UUIDs submitted to `POST /api/v1/admin/object-links:purge`. Submissions with fewer than 1 or more than 200 items are rejected with HTTP 422.\n- **`reason`:** The mandatory rationale string required by `POST /api/v1/admin/object-links:purge`. The string must contain at least 10 non-whitespace characters (e.g. `\"Decommissioning orphaned test links post-cutover\"`). Rationale strings containing fewer than 10 characters are rejected with HTTP 422.\n- **`status_filter`:** Selects the display subset in the Studio UI: `unpurged` (flagged items requiring operator intervention), `purged` (historically purged records), `resolved` (links where the underlying anomaly was repaired), or `all`.\n- **`immutable_audit_ledger`:** Every completed purge operation writes an immutable `INVALID_OBJECT_LINK_PURGED` event to `activity_records`. The response returns `purged_count` and the list of `activity_ids` emitted to the ledger for forensic verification.\n"
++    "content": "# [Informational] Studio Configuration Reference\n\nAuthoritative technical reference specification for all configuration attributes, execution tiers, and administrative controls across all 14 \ud83d\udc4dkanban Studios.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Workspace Studio\nThe Workspace Studio (`/workspaces/:ws/settings` or `/admin/workspace-settings`) manages top-level tenant policies, display metadata, session lifetimes, trash retention, export permissions, and air-gap network boundaries under strict AAL2 authentication.\n\n![Workspace Settings General - Desktop Light](images/slice5-workspace-settings-general-desktop-1440x900-light.png)\n![Workspace Settings General - Desktop Dark](images/slice5-workspace-settings-general-desktop-1440x900-dark.png)\n![Workspace Settings General - Tablet Landscape](images/slice5-workspace-settings-general-tablet-landscape-1024x768.png)\n![Workspace Settings General - Tablet Portrait](images/slice5-workspace-settings-general-tablet-portrait-768x1024.png)\n![Workspace Settings General - Mobile Portrait](images/slice5-workspace-settings-general-mobile-portrait-390x844.png)\n![Workspace Settings Nested Groups](images/slice5-workspace-settings-groups-nested-desktop-1440x900-light.png)\n![Workspace Settings Trash Retention](images/slice5-workspace-settings-trash-retention-desktop-1440x900-light.png)\n\n### Workspace Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `name` | Canonical internal name of the workspace | UTF-8 String (1 to 200 characters) | Required at provisioning | Tenant root identifier in PostgreSQL RLS context |\n| `slug` | URL-safe workspace slug used in REST routing and browser paths | Lowercase alphanumeric ASCII with hyphens (2 to 63 chars) | Derived from name | Immutable tenant boundary in database session context |\n| `display_name` | Human-readable workspace title displayed in navigation header | UTF-8 String (1 to 80 characters) | `\"Workspace\"` | Visible across topbar, breadcrumbs, and notification emails |\n| `default_project_visibility` | Baseline privacy level assigned to newly initialized projects | `\"private\"`, `\"workspace\"` | `\"workspace\"` | Prevents accidental cross-project disclosure in multi-team workspaces |\n| `session_idle_timeout_min` | Inactivity window before user session transitions to stale | Integer between 5 and 720 minutes | 60 | Enforces local re-authentication after inactivity period |\n| `session_absolute_timeout_min` | Maximum lifetime of an authentication session cookie | Integer between 60 and 10080 minutes | 1440 (24h) | Requires full re-login upon absolute expiration |\n| `trash_retention_days` | Duration soft-deleted items remain in Trash before permanent purge | Integer between 7 and 365 days | 30 | Governs compliance data retention and recovery windows |\n| `mcp_key_max_ttl_days` | Maximum validity period permitted for minted MCP API keys | Integer between 1 and 365 days | 90 | Caps autonomous agent credential lifetimes |\n| `air_gap_mode` | Strict isolation mode blocking external cloud AI and external egress | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `false` | When active, restricts AI execution strictly to local Ollama and on-prem runners |\n| `default_skip_loaded_sequence_numbers` | Realtime WebSocket event optimization toggle | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `false` | Skips already-cached sequence events to minimize client bandwidth |\n| `issue_export_enabled` | Allows exporting work items to JSON or CSV archives | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `true` | Controlled data exfiltration prevention |\n| `issue_export_allowed_roles` | Explicit RBAC roles permitted to export work item data | List of role strings (`\"workspace_admin\"`, `\"project_admin\"`, `\"member\"`) | All 3 roles | Restricts bulk export capability to authorized team members |\n| `issue_import_enabled` | Allows lossless ingestion from external trackers | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `true` | Gates administrative bulk ingestion workflows |\n| `report_export_enabled` | Allows exporting statistical flow reports and audit logs | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `true` | Governs access to compliance and telemetry exports |\n| `report_export_allowed_roles` | Roles allowed to download statistical flow telemetry | List of role strings (`\"ADMIN\"`, `\"MEMBER\"`) | Both roles | Prevents unauthorized aggregation of engineering velocity data |\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs (Workspace Settings)\n\n- **name:** Internal database name representing the workspace tenant in PostgreSQL. Required during bootstrap and immutable across standard operation, binding the tenant scope for Row-Level Security policies.\n- **slug:** URL-safe ASCII slug identifying the tenant in REST API endpoints (`/api/v1/workspaces/{slug}`) and browser URL routes (`/{slug}/projects`). Must be between 2 and 63 alphanumeric characters and hyphens.\n- **display_name:** The human-facing label rendered on topbars, page document titles, mobile shells, and notification cards. Supports UTF-8 strings up to 80 characters with full internationalization.\n- **default_project_visibility:** Sets the default privacy tier assigned when users create new projects. Options are `\"workspace\"` (visible to all authenticated workspace members) or `\"private\"` (accessible strictly to explicitly invited members and project administrators).\n- **session_idle_timeout_min:** Controls how long an idle user session remains active before requiring step-up authentication or PIN challenge. Configurable from 5 minutes to 720 minutes (12 hours), with a standard enterprise default of 60 minutes.\n- **session_absolute_timeout_min:** Maximum wall-clock lifespan of an authenticated session cookie regardless of user activity. Must fall between 60 minutes (1 hour) and 10080 minutes (7 days), defaulting to 1440 minutes (24 hours).\n- **trash_retention_days:** Defines how many days soft-deleted work items, docs, stickies, and cycles reside in the Trash Studio (`/trash`) before being permanently purged by the background vacuum worker. Values range from 7 to 365 days (default 30 days).\n- **mcp_key_max_ttl_days:** Upper boundary enforced when administrators or developers mint Model Context Protocol (MCP) scoped tokens. The backend rejects any key creation request specifying a TTL exceeding this configured cap (1 to 365 days, default 90 days).\n- **air_gap_mode:** When set to `true`, \ud83d\udc4dkanban activates zero-external-egress enforcement. All outbound requests to cloud AI providers (OpenAI, Gemini) and remote webhooks are fail-closed blocked at the transport layer, restricting agent pipelines exclusively to LAN Ollama servers and local CLI runners.\n- **default_skip_loaded_sequence_numbers:** An optimization flag for high-throughput teams. When enabled, WebSocket clients receiving state updates skip downloading historical activity sequence packets that the client has already recorded in IndexedDB.\n- **issue_export_enabled:** Master toggle for work item data extraction. Disabling this flag blocks `/api/v1/projects/{project}/issues/export` across both the REST API and the web interface to prevent bulk data exfiltration.\n- **issue_export_allowed_roles:** Role allowlist determining who can trigger issue exports. Configurable with combinations of `\"workspace_admin\"`, `\"project_admin\"`, and `\"member\"`.\n- **issue_import_enabled:** Administrative toggle governing data ingestion from external platforms (Plane, Jira, GitHub, Linear). Disabling this blocks `/api/v1/admin/imports` to preserve data integrity during audit periods.\n- **report_export_enabled:** Controls whether statistical flow telemetry, Monte Carlo lead-time forecasts, and velocity percentiles can be downloaded as CSV or JSON payloads.\n- **report_export_allowed_roles:** Specifies the minimum permission tier required to export engineering flow reports (options include `\"ADMIN\"` and `\"MEMBER\"`).\n\n### Step-by-Step Guide: Configuring Workspace Policies and Trash Retention\n\n1. **Access Workspace Settings:** Click your avatar or the settings cog in the top navigation bar and select **Workspace Settings** (or navigate directly to `/workspaces/<slug>/settings`).\n2. **Review General Policies:** In the **General** tab, update your **Display Name**, verify the **Workspace Slug**, and select the **Default Project Visibility** (`Workspace` or `Private`).\n3. **Configure Session Security:**\n   - Under **Session Lifetimes**, set **Session Idle Timeout** (e.g. 60 minutes) to force re-authentication for unattended terminals.\n   - Set **Session Absolute Timeout** (e.g. 1440 minutes / 24 hours) to mandate periodic credential rotation.\n4. **Tune Compliance & Trash Retention:**\n   - Navigate to the **Data Lifecycle & Trash** card.\n   - Adjust **Trash Retention Days** between 7 and 365 days (default 30 days).\n   - Soft-deleted work items remain recoverable in `/trash` for this duration. Once this window elapses, the automated cleanup daemon purges the records irreversibly.\n5. **Manage Air-Gap and Export Restrictions:**\n   - If operating in a sovereign or disconnected environment, toggle **Air-Gap Mode** to `ON` to strictly restrict all agent and runner execution to LAN hosts.\n   - Review **Allowed Export Roles** to ensure only authorized administrators and team leads can extract bulk project archives.\n6. **Save Changes:** Click **`Save Settings`**. Changes are validated, logged in the immutable audit ledger, and take effect across the workspace immediately.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Project Studio & Personal Settings\nThe Project Studio (`/projects/:proj/settings`) configures project workflows, custom status states, WIP limits, priority emojis, labels, and two-stage project deletion. Personal Settings (`/settings` or `/profile`) manages user profiles, canonical timezones, custom avatars, password rotation, Passkeys, TOTP 2FA, recovery codes, and external identity linking.\n\n![Project Settings Priorities - Desktop Light](images/slice5-project-settings-priorities-desktop-1440x900-light.png)\n![Project Settings Priorities - Desktop Dark](images/slice5-project-settings-priorities-desktop-1440x900-dark.png)\n![Project Settings Priorities - Tablet Landscape](images/slice5-project-settings-priorities-tablet-landscape-1024x768.png)\n![Project Settings Priorities - Tablet Portrait](images/slice5-project-settings-priorities-tablet-portrait-768x1024.png)\n![Project Settings Priorities - Mobile Portrait](images/slice5-project-settings-priorities-mobile-portrait-390x844.png)\n![Project Settings WIP Limits - Desktop Light](images/slice5-project-settings-wip-limits-desktop-1440x900-light.png)\n![Project Settings WIP Limits - Desktop Dark](images/slice5-project-settings-wip-limits-desktop-1440x900-dark.png)\n![Project Settings WIP Limits - Tablet Landscape](images/slice5-project-settings-wip-limits-tablet-landscape-1024x768.png)\n![Project Settings WIP Limits - Tablet Portrait](images/slice5-project-settings-wip-limits-tablet-portrait-768x1024.png)\n![Project Settings WIP Limits - Mobile Portrait](images/slice5-project-settings-wip-limits-mobile-portrait-390x844.png)\n![Project Settings Danger Zone - Desktop Light](images/slice5-project-settings-danger-zone-desktop-1440x900-light.png)\n![Project Settings Danger Zone - Desktop Dark](images/slice5-project-settings-danger-zone-desktop-1440x900-dark.png)\n![Project Settings Danger Zone - Tablet Landscape](images/slice5-project-settings-danger-zone-tablet-landscape-1024x768.png)\n![Project Settings Danger Zone - Tablet Portrait](images/slice5-project-settings-danger-zone-tablet-portrait-768x1024.png)\n![Project Settings Danger Zone - Mobile Portrait](images/slice5-project-settings-danger-zone-mobile-portrait-390x844.png)\n\n### Project Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `name` | Human-readable project display name | UTF-8 String (1 to 128 characters) | Required at creation | Scoped to workspace; surfaced in topbars and command palette |\n| `key` | Issue identifier prefix (e.g. `KAN`, `CORE`) | Uppercase ASCII alphanumeric (2 to 10 chars) | Required at creation | Immutable; generates unique issue keys across all surfaces |\n| `state_name` | Name of workflow state (e.g. `In Progress`) | UTF-8 String (1 to 64 characters) | Required | Displayed on board columns, list groupings, and peek drawers |\n| `group` | Lifecycle category mapping | `\"backlog\"`, `\"unstarted\"`, `\"started\"`, `\"completed\"`, `\"cancelled\"` | Required | Drives cycle velocity metrics, burn-downs, and SLA calculations |\n| `color_hex` | Hexadecimal color accent for state badge | 7-character hex code (`^#[0-9A-Fa-f]{6}$`) | `#3B82F6` | Provides distinct visual identification across board and table views |\n| `sort_order` | Execution ordering of states across the board | Integer (0 to 1000) | `0` | Controls horizontal board lane arrangement and transition flow |\n| `is_default` | Default state assigned to newly created issues | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `false` | Exactly one state per project can be designated default |\n| `wip_soft_limit` | Warning threshold for work-in-progress cards | Integer between 1 and 999 (or `null`) | `null` (None) | Renders subtle amber capacity warnings when column count is exceeded |\n| `wip_hard_limit` | Strict blocking limit for work-in-progress cards | Integer between 1 and 999 (or `null`) | `null` (None) | Disallows moving or creating cards into lane when limit is reached |\n| `priority_slug` | Normalized priority key (e.g. `urgent`, `high`) | Lowercase regex `^[a-z][a-z0-9_-]{0,31}$` | Required | Standardized priority value used in query filters and formula AST |\n| `priority_label` | Human-readable priority label with emoji | UTF-8 String (1 to 64 characters) | Required | Displayed on card badges, priority dropdowns, and peek rails |\n| `exclusive_group` | Mutual exclusion category for label grouping | Lowercase regex `^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9_-]{0,31}$` | `null` (Optional) | Enforces single-selection among conflicting tags (e.g. `env:prod`) |\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs (Project Settings)\n\n- **name:** The project name displayed across navigation breadcrumbs, issue peek drawers, and workspace summary cards. Scoped strictly to the active workspace.\n- **key:** The immutable uppercase prefix key identifying work items within this project (e.g. `KAN` generates `KAN-1`, `KAN-2`). Must be between 2 and 10 alphanumeric characters.\n- **state_name:** The human-facing title of a workflow state (e.g. `Backlog`, `In Progress`, `Code Review`, `Done`). Renaming a state immediately reflects across all active cards without modifying historical audit events.\n- **group:** The canonical lifecycle category to which the state belongs (`backlog`, `unstarted`, `started`, `completed`, `cancelled`). Group mapping is fundamental: cycle burndowns, cumulative flow diagrams, and throughput percentiles aggregate work based on group identity.\n- **color_hex:** A 7-character hexadecimal color string (`#RRGGBB`) applied to column lane headers, state pills, and board drop zones to ensure high contrast and immediate readability.\n- **sort_order:** An integer specifying the left-to-right presentation order of board columns and workflow dropdowns. Lower numbers appear earlier in the delivery lifecycle.\n- **is_default:** Designates the initial workflow state automatically assigned when a contributor quick-creates an issue without specifying a state. Exactly one state per project can be default.\n- **wip_soft_limit:** An advisory Work-In-Progress limit for Kanban columns (1 to 999). When the active card count exceeds this number, the column header displays an amber count badge (`5/4 WIP`) without preventing drops.\n- **wip_hard_limit:** A strict Work-In-Progress enforcement threshold (1 to 999). When reached, further card drops or status changes targeting this column are rejected at the API boundary (`422 WIP_LIMIT_EXCEEDED`). Must be greater than or equal to `wip_soft_limit`.\n- **priority_slug:** Machine-readable identifier for priority tiers (`urgent`, `high`, `medium`, `low`, `none`). Used in Safe Formula evaluations, MCP tool queries, and REST search filters.\n- **priority_label:** The user-facing priority string, typically combining an expressive semantic emoji and clear text (e.g. `\ud83d\udd34 Urgent`, `\ud83d\udfe0 High`, `\ud83d\udfe1 Medium`, `\ud83d\udd35 Low`).\n- **exclusive_group:** An optional label category constraint. When applied to tags (e.g. `tier:free`, `tier:pro`), applying one tag from the group automatically removes any previously attached tags in that same exclusive group.\n\n### Step-by-Step Guide: Setting Project WIP Limits and Workflow States\n\n1. **Navigate to Project Settings:** Open your target project (e.g. `/projects/KAN`) and click **Settings** in the project sidebar.\n2. **Open Workflow States Studio:** Select the **States** tab to view all currently configured workflow states grouped by lifecycle phase.\n3. **Add or Edit a State:**\n   - Click **`+ Add State`** or click the edit icon on an existing state row.\n   - Specify the **State Name** (e.g. `In Review`).\n   - Assign the **Lifecycle Group** (`started` for active work, `completed` for resolved work).\n   - Choose a distinct **Accent Color**.\n   - Check **Make Default** if newly created tickets should land in this column.\n4. **Configure WIP Limits:**\n   - Enter a **Soft WIP Limit** (e.g. `4`) to flag column congestion during daily standups.\n   - Enter a **Hard WIP Limit** (e.g. `6`) if your team enforces strict pull-based flow and prohibits card overloading.\n   - Verify that `wip_soft_limit <= wip_hard_limit`.\n5. **Reorder Board Columns:** Drag states up and down using the grip handle to adjust their execution sort order from left to right.\n6. **Save Changes:** Click **`Save States`** to persist the configuration. Board columns update immediately across all connected WebSockets.\n\n---\n\n### Personal Settings\nThe Personal Settings Studio (`/settings` or `/profile`) provides comprehensive user identity self-service, including canonical timezone localization, avatar upload with automatic sanitization and cropping, password rotation with server-admin entropy rules, WebAuthn Passkeys, TOTP two-factor authentication, one-time emergency recovery codes, and external OIDC/LDAP identity account linking.\n\n![Personal Settings - Desktop Light](images/slice5-personal-settings-desktop-1440x900-light.png)\n![Personal Settings - Desktop Dark](images/slice5-personal-settings-desktop-1440x900-dark.png)\n![Personal Settings - Tablet Landscape](images/slice5-personal-settings-tablet-landscape-1024x768.png)\n![Personal Settings - Tablet Portrait](images/slice5-personal-settings-tablet-portrait-768x1024.png)\n![Personal Settings - Mobile Portrait](images/slice5-personal-settings-mobile-portrait-390x844.png)\n\n### Personal Settings Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `display_name` | Public user profile name displayed to colleagues | UTF-8 String (1 to 120 characters) | User's full name | Sanitized against invisible Unicode characters and control codes |\n| `timezone` | Canonical IANA timezone used for date formatting | 15 canonical timezones (e.g. `America/New_York`, `UTC`) | `UTC` | Localizes ticket creation, audit timestamps, and SLA due date displays |\n| `avatar` | User profile photograph or custom avatar | PNG, JPEG, or WebP image file (<= 2 MiB) | Default letter icon | Re-encoded server-side via Pillow; SVG uploads rejected fail-closed |\n| `current_password` | Verification of existing credentials before password update | String matching current password | Required on change | Prevents unauthorized password changes on unattended sessions |\n| `new_password` | Updated local authentication password | Min 12 chars (Min 14 for Server Admins) | User-supplied | Salted and hashed via Argon2id with server-side pepper |\n| `passkeys` | FIDO2 / WebAuthn biometric security keys | Platform (Touch ID/Hello) or Cross-Platform (YubiKey) | None enrolled | Hardware-bound cryptographic public key authentication |\n| `totp_enabled` | Time-based one-time password (RFC 6238) 2FA | 6-digit verification code from authenticator app | Disabled | Requires AAL2 step-up challenge to disable once activated |\n| `recovery_codes` | Single-use emergency account recovery backup codes | 10 alphanumeric single-use codes | Generated on 2FA setup | Stored hashed in PostgreSQL; invalidated upon individual consumption |\n| `linked_identities` | External OIDC, Google, GitHub, or LDAP accounts | Verified external provider identity claims | Primary login card | Enables single-sign-on without password storage; unlinking requires AAL2 |\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs (Personal Settings)\n\n- **display_name:** The user's public identity rendered across comments, avatar badges, activity feeds, and assignment menus. Cleaned server-side using Unicode guards to strip zero-width spaces and spoofing homoglyphs.\n- **timezone:** The user's localized timezone selection, chosen from 15 canonical IANA timezones (such as `America/New_York`, `Europe/London`, `Asia/Tokyo`, `UTC`). All timestamps remain stored canonically as UTC in PostgreSQL and are formatted to the user's local offset on the client.\n- **avatar:** Profile picture uploaded via `PUT /api/v1/auth/me/avatar`. The backend enforces a strict 2 MiB size cap and validates the raster payload via content-sniffing. Dangerous vector formats (SVG) are rejected fail-closed to eliminate XSS risks, and images are re-encoded to sanitized PNG format.\n- **current_password:** Required whenever a user modifies their local password via `POST /api/v1/auth/me/password`. Guards against session hijacking by requiring proof of current knowledge before modifying credentials.\n- **new_password:** The updated password. The platform enforces strict entropy requirements: standard users must supply at least 12 characters, while users possessing the `SERVER_ADMIN` role must supply at least 14 characters.\n- **passkeys:** WebAuthn credentials registered via `POST /api/v1/auth/webauthn/register:start` and `:finish`. Passkeys support both platform biometrics (Touch ID, Face ID, Windows Hello) and cross-platform hardware tokens (YubiKey). Each enrolled key displays nickname, enrolled date, and a non-intrusive **Test Passkey** verification button.\n- **totp_enabled:** Activates Time-Based One-Time Password verification for authenticator applications (Aegis, Google Authenticator, 1Password). Enrolled via QR code and verified with a 6-digit challenge code. Disabling TOTP requires mandatory AAL2 step-up verification.\n- **recovery_codes:** A set of 10 single-use emergency recovery codes generated when two-factor authentication is configured. Each code can be used exactly once to regain account access if hardware tokens or authenticator apps are lost.\n- **linked_identities:** Manages connected third-party enterprise identity cards (Google Workspace, GitHub, GitLab, Okta, Active Directory LDAP). Users can link multiple external accounts to a single local identity or unlink secondary providers after completing AAL2 step-up verification.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Workflow Transition Studio & Git Repo Bindings\nThe Workflow Transition Studio (`/projects/:proj/settings/workflows`) coordinates lifecycle rules, pre-transition validators, field mutators, asynchronous AI agent gates, and read-only bare Git repository mirror bindings.\n\n![Workflow Transition Studio - Desktop Dark](images/slice5-workflow-studio-desktop-1440x900-dark.png)\n![Workflow Transition Studio - Tablet Landscape](images/slice5-workflow-studio-tablet-landscape-1024x768.png)\n![Workflow Transition Studio - Tablet Portrait](images/slice5-workflow-studio-tablet-portrait-768x1024.png)\n![Workflow Transition Studio - Mobile Portrait](images/slice5-workflow-studio-mobile-portrait-390x844.png)\n\n### Workflow Transition Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `name` | Descriptive label for transition helper hook | UTF-8 String (1 to 128 characters) | Required | Displayed in pipeline lists, dry-run logs, and error explanations |\n| `from_state` | Origin workflow state of the transition rule | Valid state name or `null` (Any state) | `null` (Wildcard) | Restricts hook evaluation to specific card movements |\n| `to_state` | Destination workflow state of the transition | Valid project state name | Required | The target column being protected by the transition helper |\n| `hook_kind` | Execution engine and evaluation timing | `\"sync_validator\"`, `\"sync_mutator\"`, `\"agent_interceptor\"` | `\"sync_validator\"` | Governs execution latency and synchronous vs asynchronous processing |\n| `failure_policy` | Action taken if helper times out or rejects | `\"fail_closed\"`, `\"fail_open\"` | `\"fail_closed\"` | Fail-closed guarantees invalid transitions never commit |\n| `wasm_module_sha256` | SHA-256 digest of sandboxed Wasm module | 64-character lowercase hex string | Required for Wasm | Verifies bytecode authenticity before in-process execution |\n| `agent` | Identifier of registered autonomous agent | Valid agent slug (e.g. `reality-checker`) | Required for Agent | Binds asynchronous gate to configured LLM or CLI runner harness |\n| `timeout_ms` | Maximum allowed execution time in milliseconds | Integer between 50 and 60000 ms | 60000 for Agent | Protects transition queues from hanging subprocesses |\n| `allowed_mutable_fields` | Explicit field allowlist for mutator hooks | List of field names (`closed_at`, `labels`, `priority`) | `[]` (Empty) | Enforces least-privilege mutation; `state` and `description` are forbidden |\n| `repo_slug` | Identifier for project bare Git mirror | Regex `^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{1,62}$` | Required | Names local bare mirror directory under `/var/lib/kanban/repos/` |\n| `remote_url` | Upstream Git repository clone URL | HTTPS or SSH Git URL | Required | Validated against SSRF policy before initiating background fetch |\n| `default_branch` | Primary branch used for agent diff inspections | Valid Git ref name (e.g. `main`, `master`) | `\"main\"` | Baseline target branch used by reality-checker audits |\n| `sync_interval_min` | Frequency of automated background mirror fetch | Integer between 5 and 1440 minutes | 15 | Keeps bare mirror synchronized with remote commits |\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs (Workflow Transition Studio & Repo Bindings)\n\n- **name:** The user-defined identifier assigned to the workflow hook (such as `qa-done-gate` or `enforce-pr-link`). Appears on pipeline transition cards, dry-run evaluation outputs, and timeline rejection entries.\n- **from_state:** The initial state of the card before transition (e.g. `In Progress`). Selecting `null` creates a wildcard rule matching transitions originating from any state into `to_state`.\n- **to_state:** The protected destination state (e.g. `Done`). The card cannot land in this state until all attached synchronous validators pass and all asynchronous agent gates approve.\n- **hook_kind:** Defines the execution engine: `\"sync_validator\"` runs fast in-process WebAssembly checks (<25ms); `\"sync_mutator\"` applies deterministic property updates; `\"agent_interceptor\"` dispatches an asynchronous background gate to an AI model or test runner.\n- **failure_policy:** The error handling invariant. `\"fail_closed\"` (default) blocks the transition immediately if the helper fails, times out, or errors. `\"fail_open\"` logs an administrative warning to the activity ledger and allows the card transition to complete.\n- **wasm_module_sha256:** The cryptographic hash of the compiled WebAssembly bytecode module executed by the `wasmtime` engine. Prevents execution of unverified or tampered binaries.\n- **agent:** The slug of the registered autonomous agent (`@reality-checker`, `@security-auditor`) assigned to evaluate asynchronous gate transitions. The attach dialog groups available agents by execution harness (`cli_pipe`, `in_app_sdk`, `remote_runner`).\n- **timeout_ms:** The maximum wall-clock execution deadline allocated to the helper. For Wasm validators, timeouts are capped at 250ms. For agent interceptors, timeouts can be configured up to 60000ms (60 seconds) to accommodate large reasoning model evaluations.\n- **allowed_mutable_fields:** A strict security allowlist restricting which issue properties a `sync_mutator` hook can modify (e.g. `closed_at`, `labels`, `priority`). Project invariants strictly forbid modifying `state` or `description_markdown` to prevent recursive transition loops and content tampering.\n- **repo_slug:** The local filesystem folder name allocated to the bare Git mirror. Must be a clean lowercase alphanumeric string with hyphens.\n- **remote_url:** The external Git repository URL (e.g. `https://github.com/org/repo.git`). The server validates the URL against an anti-SSRF policy (blocking RFC 1918 private IP addresses unless explicitly allowlisted) before establishing outbound connections.\n- **default_branch:** The primary branch name against which code inspection scripts, PR validation checks, and diff analyses are evaluated.\n- **sync_interval_min:** Interval in minutes between background mirror fetch operations. The daemon executes `git fetch --prune` inside the bare mirror to maintain parity with remote pushes.\n\n### Step-by-Step Guide: Attaching Workflow Transition Hooks with Dry-Run\n\n1. **Navigate to Workflow Studio:** Open `/projects/<PROJECT_KEY>/settings/workflows`.\n2. **Select Transition Rule:** Locate the transition rule card for your target state movement (e.g. `In Progress -> Done`).\n3. **Click Attach Hook:** Click the **`+ Attach Hook`** button to open the **Attach Workflow Hook Dialog**.\n4. **Define Hook Parameters:**\n   - **Name:** Enter a unique label (e.g. `reality-checker-done-gate`).\n   - **Hook Kind:** Select `sync_validator` (Wasm), `sync_mutator` (Wasm), or `agent_interceptor` (Async Agent Gate).\n   - **Failure Policy:** Choose `Fail-Closed` to guarantee that unverified tickets cannot enter the protected state.\n5. **Select Engine or Agent:**\n   - For Wasm: Choose an approved module (e.g. `check-blockers-resolved`).\n   - For Agent Interceptor: Select the target **Agent Definition** from the dropdown. The system automatically reads the agent's real `timeout_ms` (e.g. `60000ms`).\n6. **Save Hook:** Click **`Attach Hook`** to commit the hook to the project's transition pipeline.\n7. **Simulate in Dry-Run Drawer:**\n   - Click the **`Dry-Run`** button on the rule card.\n   - Choose a sample ticket fixture or enter a live issue key.\n   - Click **`Run Dry-Run`** to execute the pipeline in a simulated, non-mutating sandbox.\n   - Inspect the execution timings (e.g. `1.2ms`, `3.4ms`), returned logs, and allowed/vetoed verdicts before deploying to production.\n\n### Step-by-Step Guide: Binding Git Repositories\n\n1. **Access Repository Bindings:** Navigate to `/projects/<PROJECT_KEY>/settings/workflows` and scroll down to the **Git Repository Bindings** section.\n2. **Click Add Repository:** Click **`+ Add Repository`** to open the repository configuration drawer.\n3. **Configure Repository Details:**\n   - **Slug:** Provide a clean identifier (e.g. `kanban-core`).\n   - **Remote URL:** Enter the remote Git repository URL.\n   - **Default Branch:** Enter the default branch (`main`).\n   - **Sync Interval:** Configure the automated polling frequency (default 15 minutes).\n4. **Enforce Read-Only Mirror Invariant:** The system creates a local bare Git mirror with strict read-only permissions (`:ro`). Autonomous agents can query commit trees and run `git diff` during gate reviews, but write access is impossible.\n5. **Trigger Immediate Sync:** Click **`Sync Now`** to perform an initial clone and verify that commit SHAs and branch references synchronize successfully.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Custom Fields Studio & Dual-Control Escalation\nThe Custom Fields Studio (`/projects/:proj/settings/custom-fields`) manages dynamic attributes, safe mathematical formulas, WebAssembly modules, and dual-control tenant scope escalations under AAL2 cryptographic verification.\n\n![Custom Fields Studio - Desktop Dark](images/slice5-custom-fields-studio-desktop-1440x900-dark.png)\n![Custom Fields Studio - Tablet Landscape](images/slice5-custom-fields-studio-tablet-landscape-1024x768.png)\n![Custom Fields Studio - Tablet Portrait](images/slice5-custom-fields-studio-tablet-portrait-768x1024.png)\n![Custom Fields Studio - Mobile Portrait](images/slice5-custom-fields-studio-mobile-portrait-390x844.png)\n![Custom Fields Editor Tier A](images/slice5-custom-fields-editor-tier-a-desktop-1440x900-light.png)\n![Custom Fields Structured Format](images/slice5-custom-fields-structured-format-desktop-1440x900-light.png)\n![Custom Fields Dual Control Escalation](images/slice5-custom-fields-escalation-dual-control-desktop-1440x900-light.png)\n\n### Custom Fields Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `name` | Human-readable custom field title | String (1 to 64 characters) | Required | Displayed on ticket rails, table columns, and board badges |\n| `slug` | Unique identifier used in formulas and API queries | Lowercase alphanumeric ASCII with hyphens | Derived from name | Used in formula expressions and API JSON properties |\n| `flavor` | Execution tier and underlying calculation engine | `\"scalar\"`, `\"formula\"`, `\"wasm\"`, `\"nsjail\"` | `\"formula\"` | Determines execution latency and Standalone mode compatibility |\n| `sandbox_tier` | Multi-tier sandbox engine used for execution | `\"scalar\"`, `\"wasmtime\"` (Tier A), `\"nsjail\"` (Tier B) | `\"wasmtime\"` | `nsjail` requires multi-container compose; fail-closed in standalone |\n| `credential_mode` | Identity token provided during script execution | `\"INVOKING_USER\"`, `\"SERVICE_ACCOUNT\"` | `\"INVOKING_USER\"` | `SERVICE_ACCOUNT` requires dual-control cryptographic approval |\n| `target_scope` | Maximum tenant visibility boundary of the field | `\"PROJECT\"`, `\"WORKSPACE\"`, `\"SERVER\"` | `\"PROJECT\"` | Escalation beyond `PROJECT` requires AAL2 administrative step-up |\n| `output_format` | Canonical schema used to format and render output | `\"text\"`, `\"number\"`, `\"badge\"`, `\"severity_pill\"`, `\"url\"`, `\"markdown_snippet\"`, `\"json_table\"` | `\"text\"` | Enforces strict Pydantic validation before persisting to JSONB |\n| `format_metadata` | Configuration options tailored to chosen format | JSON object matching format schema | `{}` | Governs decimal precision, color tokens, allowlists, or table headers |\n| `script_source` | Raw source code for formula or script evaluation | Python AST expression, Rust Wasm, or script | Required | Signed with SHA-256 digest upon commit; immutable against database tampering |\n| `egress_allowlist` | Allowed destination hostnames for nsjail tier | Comma-separated domain list | Empty (Zero egress) | Network disabled by default via Linux kernel `CLONE_NEWNET` |\n| `approval_status` | Current dual-control escalation state | `\"pending\"`, `\"approved\"`, `\"rejected\"`, `\"revoked\"` | `\"approved\"` (Project) | Gated at `pending` when requesting Workspace or Server scope |\n| `aal2_freshness` | Maximum age of AAL2 verification for approval | Integer (Max 900 seconds / 15 minutes) | 900s | Enforces fresh multi-factor authentication on administrative step-up |\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs (Custom Fields Studio & Dual-Control Escalation)\n\n- **name:** The human-facing label displayed on ticket property rails, table view columns, and board card badges. Rendered alongside a subtle test tube icon (`\ud83e\uddea`) for computed fields.\n- **slug:** The immutable ASCII identifier representing the custom field in JSON payloads, query filters, and Safe Formula references. Derived automatically from the field name upon creation.\n- **flavor:** The computation tier. `\"scalar\"` stores static values; `\"formula\"` executes deterministic Python AST math in-process (<5ms); `\"wasm\"` runs compiled WebAssembly modules via `wasmtime` (<25ms); `\"nsjail\"` executes Linux container scripts (<250ms).\n- **sandbox_tier:** Selects between in-process WebAssembly (`wasmtime` Tier A) and containerized namespace isolation (`nsjail` Tier B). In Standalone mode, `nsjail` is fail-closed disabled with `SANDBOX_UNAVAILABLE_IN_STANDALONE`.\n- **credential_mode:** Determines which identity credentials are provided to the script during execution. `\"INVOKING_USER\"` executes strictly within the permissions of the user viewing or updating the card. `\"SERVICE_ACCOUNT\"` grants elevated service identity and mandates dual-control approval.\n- **target_scope:** Controls the data visibility boundary of the field. `\"PROJECT\"` scope can only read issues within the active project. Escalating to `\"WORKSPACE\"` (reading across all workspace projects) or `\"SERVER\"` (global) requires dual-control cryptographic sign-off.\n- **output_format:** The strict Pydantic schema used to validate and render the field's computed output. Supports 7 canonical formats: `text`, `number`, `badge`, `severity_pill`, `url`, `markdown_snippet`, and `json_table`.\n- **format_metadata:** A JSON configuration dictionary customizing the chosen output format (such as specifying `unit_suffix: \"%\"`, `maximum_fraction_digits: 1`, or table column definitions).\n- **script_source:** The executable source code (Python formula expression, Wasm bytecode, or shell script). Hashed with SHA-256 upon save; if the hash in PostgreSQL fails verification at runtime, execution is halted immediately.\n- **egress_allowlist:** A comma-separated list of permitted network hosts accessible to `nsjail` scripts. By default, network egress is completely disabled via kernel `CLONE_NEWNET`.\n- **approval_status:** Tracks the dual-control lifecycle. Fields requesting `WORKSPACE` or `SERVER` scope enter `pending` status and cannot execute until approved by an administrator holding target tier authority.\n- **aal2_freshness:** The security policy requiring that the approving administrator must have performed multi-factor authentication (biometric Passkey, hardware key, or TOTP) within the preceding 900 seconds (15 minutes).\n\n### Step-by-Step Guide: Authoring and Requesting Dual-Control Custom Scripted Fields\n\n1. **Open Custom Fields Studio:** Navigate to `/projects/<PROJECT_KEY>/settings/custom-fields` and click **`+ New Custom Field`**.\n2. **Define Field Metadata:**\n   - Enter a **Name** (e.g. `Cross-Project Security Compliance Score`).\n   - Confirm the automatically generated **Slug** (`cross-proj-sec-score`).\n   - Select the **Flavor** (`formula` or `wasm`).\n   - Select the **Output Format** (`severity_pill` or `number`).\n3. **Request Elevated Scope:**\n   - Set **Target Scope** to `WORKSPACE` or `SERVER`.\n   - Optionally toggle **Credential Mode** to `SERVICE_ACCOUNT`.\n   - The UI immediately surfaces an amber **Dual-Control Escalation Banner** warning of the expanded data access boundary.\n4. **Draft and Test Execute:**\n   - Author your formula expression or script logic.\n   - Click **`Test Execute`** to simulate evaluation in memory against sample payloads.\n5. **Submit for Approval:** Click **`Save & Request Escalation`**. The field enters `pending` approval status.\n6. **Independent AAL2 Review & Approval:**\n   - A distinct Workspace or Server Administrator opens the Custom Fields Studio.\n   - The reviewer inspects script source, requested scope, and blast radius.\n   - The reviewer clicks **`Approve Escalation`** and provides fresh Authenticator Assurance Level 2 (AAL2) credentials (Passkey, security key, or TOTP).\n   - Once verified, the field is activated and logged in the immutable audit ledger.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 5. Authentication Studio\nThe Multi-Card Authentication Studio (`/admin/auth`) configures enterprise identity providers, WebAuthn Passkeys, LDAP trees, and break-glass recovery.\n\n\n### Authentication Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `provider_type` | Underlying identity protocol card | `LOCAL_ADMIN`, `OAUTH2`, `OIDC`, `LDAP`, `WEBAUTHN`, `TOTP` | Required | Determines credential validation handshake and token exchange |\n| `card_enabled` | Master active toggle for the authentication card | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `true` | Disabled cards reject authentication attempts immediately |\n| `sort_rank` | Order in which login cards appear on `/login` screen | Non-negative integer (lower ranks appear first) | 10 | Allows organizations to prioritize SSO while preserving local admin cards |\n| `rp_name` | WebAuthn Relying Party display name | String (Always canonical `\ud83d\udc4dkanban`) | `\ud83d\udc4dkanban` | Displayed by browser and hardware security keys during passkey prompts |\n| `rp_id` | WebAuthn Relying Party domain identifier | Strict ASCII FQDN without protocol or port | Hostname of deployment | Must match browser origin to prevent credential theft or spoofing |\n| `issuer_url` | OIDC discovery endpoint URL | Valid HTTPS URL | None | Fetches `/.well-known/openid-configuration` and cryptographic keys |\n| `client_id` | OAuth/OIDC application client identifier | ASCII string | None | Public identifier registered with enterprise identity provider |\n| `client_secret` | OAuth/OIDC client secret credential | Encrypted string (write-only custody) | None | Stored encrypted in PostgreSQL; never exposed over REST API |\n| `ldap_host` | LDAPS directory hostname or IP address | Hostname or IP | None | LDAPS on port 636 or StartTLS required; plaintext LDAP rejected |\n| `ldap_bind_dn` | Distinguished Name used for directory lookup | LDAP DN string (e.g. `cn=admin,dc=corp,dc=com`) | None | Service account with read-only search permissions across user tree |\n| `break_glass_active` | Emergency lockout prevention bypass trigger | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `false` | Allows emergency local admin access when SSO is offline |\n\n---\n\n## 6. MCP Access Studio\nThe MCP Access Studio (`/settings/mcp` or `/admin/mcp`) manages Model Context Protocol API tokens, Level of Detail (LOD) defaults, and rate limits.\n\n\n### MCP Access Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `surface_type` | Target isolated streamable endpoint | `/mcp/agent`, `/mcp/docs`, `/mcp/admin` | `/mcp/agent` | Determines tool catalog and token budget ceiling (<= 1,850 tokens for agent) |\n| `key_name` | Descriptive name of the API token or client tool | String (1 to 64 characters) | Required | Surfaced in audit logs and active session inspector |\n| `token_prefix` | Standardized key prefix identifying privilege level | `kb_agt_*`, `kb_doc_*`, `kb_adm_*` | Auto-assigned | Cryptographically identifies target surface before database lookup |\n| `lod_tier` | Default Level of Detail returned in tool responses | `summary`, `standard`, `full` | `standard` | Controls response verbosity; `summary` minimizes agent context consumption |\n| `rate_limit_rpm` | Maximum allowed tool invocations per minute | Integer between 10 and 600 | 120 | Protects database connection pool from runaway autonomous agent loops |\n| `expires_at` | Expiration timestamp for the API key | ISO-8601 Timestamp or `None` | 90 days from creation | Expired keys reject calls with `401 UNAUTHORIZED` |\n| `destructive_gate` | Enables two-stage confirmation for destructive operations | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `true` | Requires short-lived confirmation token before permanent deletions |\n| `capability_mask` | Operational capability bitmask ceiling | Integer bitmask (1=View, 3=Comment, 31=Member, 255=Admin) | 255 (Full) | Monotonic `CredentialBoundary` ceiling; mask 1 restricts scopes to `${surface}:read` |\n| `allowed_cidrs` | Subnet IP allowlist for incoming MCP invocations | Comma or whitespace separated IPv4/IPv6 CIDRs | None (Allow all) | Rejects calls originating outside specified subnets with `403 FORBIDDEN_CIDR` |\n\n---\n\n## 7. OAuth 2.1 Governance Studio\nThe OAuth 2.1 Governance Studio (`/admin/oauth`) registers client applications, enforces PKCE code challenges, and configures token lifetimes.\n\n\n### OAuth 2.1 Governance Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `client_name` | Name of the integrating client application | String (1 to 128 characters) | Required | Displayed on user authorization consent screens |\n| `client_id` | Unique OAuth client identifier | String prefixed with `kb_client_` | Auto-generated | Public application key used during authorization code flow |\n| `redirect_uris` | Allowed callback URLs for authorization codes | List of strict HTTPS URLs (localhost allowed for dev) | Required | Exact string matching enforced; wildcards strictly forbidden |\n| `pkce_enforced` | Proof Key for Code Exchange requirement | Mandatory `S256` challenge | `S256` | Rejects plaintext code challenges; mitigates authorization code interception |\n| `allowed_scopes` | List of privilege scopes client can request | `agent`, `docs`, `admin`, `read:issues`, `write:issues` | `agent` | Token capability bitmask cannot exceed granted scopes |\n| `access_token_ttl` | Lifetime of minted access tokens | Integer seconds (300 to 86400) | 3600 (1 hour) | Ephemeral lifetime limiting blast radius of token leakage |\n| `refresh_token_ttl` | Lifetime of rotating refresh tokens | Integer seconds (86400 to 2592000) | 2592000 (30 days) | Refresh tokens rotate upon use; reused tokens revoke family |\n\n---\n\n## 8. AI Providers Studio\nThe AI Providers Studio (`/admin/ai`) registers model providers, configures write-only API keys, and sets monthly token consumption caps.\n\n\n### AI Providers Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `provider_key` | Underlying AI provider implementation | `openai`, `gemini`, `ollama`, `custom` | `gemini` | Selects prompt serializer, tool calling dialect, and streaming pipeline |\n| `priority` | Fallback dispatch order when multiple providers are active | Integer (1 to 100, higher runs first) | 10 | Primary provider attempted first; falls back on rate limit or 5xx |\n| `api_endpoint` | Base URL for API communications | Valid HTTP/HTTPS URL | Official cloud endpoint | Local Ollama uses `http://localhost:11434`; cloud requires HTTPS |\n| `api_key` | Cryptographic API secret for provider access | String (Write-only custody) | None | Stored encrypted in PostgreSQL; never sent back to browser client |\n| `monthly_token_cap` | Monthly token consumption budget ceiling | Non-negative integer (0 = unlimited) | 1,000,000 | Blocks cloud invocations once budget is reached to prevent cost overruns |\n| `default_model` | Model identifier used for sparkle and transition gates | String (e.g. `gemini-2.5-flash`, `gpt-4o`) | Provider default | Balances reasoning capability against response latency |\n| `temperature` | Randomness parameter for model generations | Float between 0.0 and 1.0 | 0.2 | Low temperatures ensure deterministic responses for code and QA gates |\n\n---\n\n## 9. Agent Definitions Studio\nThe Agent Definitions Studio (`/admin/agents`) authors system personas, binds autonomous agents to service accounts, and configures execution harnesses.\n\n\n### Agent Definitions Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `agent_name` | Unique name and identifier for the autonomous agent | String (1 to 64 characters) | Required | Used in ticket mentions, activity streams, and assignment cards |\n| `system_prompt` | Core instructions, persona guidelines, and security boundaries | UTF-8 Markdown text | Required | Directs model reasoning; stripped of dangerous injection sequences |\n| `execution_harness` | Runtime environment executing agent invocations | `api_direct`, `cli_pipe`, `remote_runner` | `api_direct` | `cli_pipe` uses zero-shell `agy -p` or `codex -p` in Linux jail |\n| `cli_pipe_binary` | Binary path used for zero-shell CLI pipe harness | Pre-approved binary name (`agy`, `codex`) | `agy` | Executes without shell or bash; communicates strictly over STDIN/STDOUT |\n| `service_principal_id` | Bound service account identity for database RLS | Valid UUID of service principal | Auto-provisioned | Binds database permissions to caller identity via `SET LOCAL` |\n| `repository_access` | Git repository access mode granted to the agent | `none`, `ro_mount`, `ephemeral_clone` | `none` | `ro_mount` mounts repository strictly read-only for forensic QA |\n\n---\n\n## 10. Remote Runners Studio\nThe Remote Runners Studio (`/admin/agent-runners`) configures distributed build nodes, mints enrollment tokens, and monitors mTLS heartbeats over dedicated port `:8444`.\n\n\n### Remote Runners Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `runner_name` | Unique hostname or identifier of the runner node | String (1 to 64 characters) | Required | Displayed in runner health dashboard and active job telemetry |\n| `enrollment_token` | Single-use cryptographic token for runner registration | Base64-encoded HMAC string | Minted on demand | Expires after 15 minutes (900 seconds max); grants certificate signing request (CSR) |\n| `mtls_cert_fingerprint`| SHA-256 fingerprint of validated client certificate | Hexadecimal SHA-256 hash | Registered upon enroll | Hardware-bound identity verifying mutual TLS WebSocket connection over port :8444 |\n| `runner_cli_command` | Standard enrollment invocation command | Shell command string | Generated on mint | Syntax: `echo \"$TOKEN\" \\| uv run kanban-agent-runner enroll --server-url <origin> --token-stdin --pool <pool>` |\n| `max_concurrent_jobs` | Maximum simultaneous task executions allowed on runner | Integer between 1 and 16 | 2 | Throttles runner workload to prevent resource exhaustion |\n| `heartbeat_timeout_s` | Maximum allowable duration without ping before offline | Integer between 10 and 120 seconds | 30 | Dead runners are marked offline and active jobs rescheduled |\n| `allowed_repo_remotes` | Whitelist of Git repository URLs runner can clone | Comma-separated list of repository URLs | Empty (All authorized) | Prevents runner exploitation for arbitrary network exfiltration |\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Runner Certificate Rotation Lifecycle:** Runner client certificates are hardware-bound and cannot be silently renewed. To rotate a certificate, revoke the runner in `/admin/agent-runners` (AAL2 challenge confirmed), mint a fresh enrollment token (15-minute TTL), and re-enroll the runner daemon.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Standalone & Promotion Studio\nThe Standalone & Promotion Studio (`/admin/standalone`) inspects deployment topologies, triggers promotion to multi-container compose, and exports encrypted backups.\n\n![Standalone Status Studio - Desktop Light](images/slice4-standalone-1440x900-light.png)\n\n\n### Standalone & Promotion Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `deployment_mode` | Current platform runtime environment | `standalone_embedded_pg`, `production_multi_container` | Detected at boot | Gates availability of nsjail container sandboxes and Redis hubs |\n| `passphrase` | Secret passphrase used to derive AES-256 backup key | String (Minimum 16 characters) | User-supplied | Encrypts archive into streaming KBKP or snapshot KBBAK1 format |\n| `auto_backup_enabled` | Recurring automated background snapshot toggle | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `false` | Instructs .kb_ops.sock daemon to create automated snapshots |\n| `backup_interval_hours`| Frequency of automated snapshot execution | Integer between 1 and 168 hours | `24` | Governs Recovery Point Objective (RPO) and background IO scheduling |\n| `snapshot_retention_count` | Number of historical snapshot archives retained on server disk | Integer between 1 and 90 | `7` | Purges older snapshots automatically while keeping latest N snapshots |\n| `confirm_overwrite` | Confirmation phrase required for destructive restore | Exact string `\"RESTORE\"` | None | Prevents accidental database erasure during snapshot restoration |\n| `backup_storage_path` | Local filesystem path where backups are stored | Directory path (`/var/lib/kanban/data/backups`) | `/var/lib/kanban/data/backups` | Isolated storage managed by `/run/postgresql/.kb_ops.sock` daemon |\n| `compose_networks` | Network isolation topology for exported compose bundle | 5 networks (`edge_net`, `app_net`, `data_net`, `sandbox_net`, `egress_llm_net`) | 5 networks | Eliminates single-network lateral movement across services |\n\n![Standalone Backups and Snapshot History](images/slice4-standalone-backups-snapshot-history.png)\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs\n\n- **`deployment_mode`:** Detects whether \ud83d\udc4dkanban is executing inside a single standalone container (`standalone_embedded_pg`, typically launched via `docker run -p 8080:8080 thumbs-up-kanban:latest` with internal PostgreSQL 16) or within a compartmentalized production cluster (`production_multi_container`). In standalone mode, external Redis is optional and background tasks run in-process, whereas multi-container mode unlocks dedicated worker containers and nsjail container isolation.\n- **`passphrase`:** The client-supplied cryptographic passphrase submitted during manual streaming backup download (`POST /api/v1/admin/standalone/backup`) and database restoration (`POST /api/v1/admin/standalone/restore`). Must be at least 16 characters in length. The server derives an AES-256-GCM symmetric key via PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 across 600,000 iterations for KBKP streaming downloads, or Argon2id for KBBAK1 archives.\n- **`auto_backup_enabled`:** Toggles automated recurring background snapshots executed by the root-privileged `/run/postgresql/.kb_ops.sock` daemon. When enabled, the daemon captures full PostgreSQL state and object graph assets into `/var/lib/kanban/data/backups` using the timestamped `KBBAK1` format without interrupting active HTTP or WebSocket connections.\n- **`backup_interval_hours`:** Configures the interval between automated snapshot runs. The backend permits values between 1 and 168 hours (default 24 hours). This setting determines how frequently the `/run/postgresql/.kb_ops.sock` daemon wakes to execute scheduled pg_dump snapshot routines.\n- **`snapshot_retention_count`:** Defines the maximum number of automated snapshot `.kbbak` archives preserved within `/var/lib/kanban/data/backups` (configurable from 1 to 90, default 7). When a newly generated snapshot exceeds this threshold, the snapshot manager unlinks the oldest archives while verifying that at least the configured retention count remains accessible.\n- **`confirm_overwrite`:** The mandatory safety gate required by the destructive database restore endpoint (`POST /api/v1/admin/standalone/restore`). The payload must contain the exact uppercase string `\"RESTORE\"`. Any request containing mismatched, lowercase, or omitted confirmation tokens is immediately rejected with HTTP 422 before any database sequence is touched or tables are dropped.\n- **`backup_storage_path`:** The server-side directory where automated `.kbbak` archives reside (`/var/lib/kanban/data/backups`). This path is protected with restrictive permissions and managed exclusively by the fixed-operation socket daemon.\n- **`compose_networks`:** The 5-network topology exported by `GET /api/v1/admin/standalone/compose-export`. Separates traffic across `edge_net` (Caddy TLS ingress), `app_net` (FastAPI backend), `data_net` (PostgreSQL 16 and Redis), `sandbox_net` (untrusted Wasm and runner execution), and `egress_llm_net` (outbound AI provider traffic). Accompanied by a secret-scrubbed `.env.example` file.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Migration Import Studio\nThe Migration Import Studio (`/admin/imports`) provides lossless data ingestion from Plane, Jira, GitHub Issues, Linear, and canonical portable workspace bundles.\n\n![Migration Import Studio Multi-Source Tabs](images/slice4-imports-multi-source-tabs.png)\n\n\n### Migration Import Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `workspace_slug` | Destination workspace tenant identifier | Lowercase alphanumeric ASCII with hyphens | Active workspace | Attaches imported entities to specified tenant under PostgreSQL RLS |\n| `project_key` | Destination project prefix key (e.g. `KAN`, `JIRA`) | Uppercase ASCII alphanumeric (2 to 10 chars) | Bundle default | Re-keys issues sequentially while preserving historical keys as aliases |\n| `dry_run` | Non-destructive simulation toggle | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `false` | Calculates entity diffs inside a database savepoint without persisting |\n| `user_map` | Mapping dictionary from source users to canonical users | JSON dictionary of strings | `{}` | Binds historical authors, assignees, and commenters to active principals |\n| `export_workspace_slug` | Target workspace for generating portable bundles | Existing workspace slug | Current | Exports canonical `.tar.gz` archive containing entities, docs, and links |\n| `export_scrub` | Redacts sensitive secrets and tokens during export | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `false` | Scrubs passwords, bearer tokens, and credentials from markdown before export |\n\n![Migration Import Studio - Desktop Light](images/slice4-imports-1440x900-light.png)\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs\n\n- **`per_source_endpoints`:** Ingestion is executed via 4 distinct per-platform endpoints: `POST /api/v1/admin/imports/plane` (Plane workspace JSON), `POST /api/v1/admin/imports/jira` (Jira Cloud/Server JSON or REST dump with ADF support), `POST /api/v1/admin/imports/github` (GitHub Issues REST/CLI JSON), and `POST /api/v1/admin/imports/linear` (Linear JSON archive). Each parser normalizes rich text into sanitized UTF-8 CommonMark while strictly rejecting Unicode lone surrogates.\n- **`workspace_slug` and `project_key`:** Defines the destination tenant context and issue key prefix for the imported dataset. If specified, issues are allocated sequential integer sequence IDs under the destination project key (e.g. `KAN-1`, `KAN-2`). If left empty, the importer provisions or resolves the workspace and project names specified within the input bundle.\n- **`dry_run`:** Controls whether database modifications are committed. When set to `true`, the importer runs within a PostgreSQL `SAVEPOINT`, parses all entities, validates foreign keys, checks user mappings, and emits an `ImportDiffReport` before rolling back the savepoint. When set to `false`, the operation executes within an atomic database transaction that commits only upon 100% validation success.\n- **`user_map`:** A JSON dictionary mapping source account identifiers, legacy usernames, or email addresses to canonical \ud83d\udc4dkanban user accounts (e.g. `{\"legacy_dev\": \"alice@example.com\"}`). Unmapped accounts fall back to system defaults, ensuring that ownership, assignments, and historical comments remain traceable.\n- **`legacy_key_aliases`:** Upstream issue keys (such as `JIRA-101`, `GH-88`, `ENG-42`) are automatically extracted and recorded in the `issue_key_aliases` catalog. When queries arrive for obsolete keys (`GET /api/v1/issues/{issue_key}`), API clients receive `200 OK` JSON with header `X-Kanban-Canonical-Key: {canonical_key}`, while browser requests requesting `text/html` receive `HTTP 308 Permanent Redirect` with `Location: /projects/{project_key}/issues/{canonical_key}`.\n- **`export_workspace_slug` and `export_scrub`:** Used by the portable workspace exporter (`POST /api/v1/admin/exports/portable`) to generate a compressed `.tar.gz` bundle. When `scrub=true` (`export_scrub` in UI), secret patterns and authentication tokens are actively redacted from prose fields before packaging.\n\n---\n\n## 13. Admin Diagnostics & Realtime Probes Studio\nThe Admin Diagnostics Studio (`/admin/diagnostics`) monitors subsystem health, executes active telemetry probes, and tracks container resource limits.\n\n![Admin Diagnostics Studio - Desktop Light](images/slice4-diagnostics-1440x900-light.png)\n\n\n### Admin Diagnostics Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `postgresql_rls` | PostgreSQL connectivity, pool saturation, migrations, RLS | 82 migrations, 100 tables `FORCE RLS` | Continuous | Core persistence and tenant isolation; latency threshold < 15ms |\n| `realtime_hub_outbox` | Transactional outbox relay and dispatch loop | Backlog depth, lag seconds, listener status | Continuous | Event delivery to WebSocket subscribers; lag threshold < 5s |\n| `job_worker_pool` | Background asynchronous task execution engine | Active tasks, worker concurrency, DLQ depth | Continuous | Processes asynchronous jobs; dead-letter queue must equal 0 |\n| `wasm_script_sandbox` | Wasmtime AST compiler and script sandbox execution | Compiler state, memory limits, fuel budget | Continuous | Sandboxed custom fields; fuel caps prevent infinite execution loops |\n| `agent_runtime_providers`| Health and latency of active AI model providers | `openai`, `gemini`, `ollama` provider status | Continuous | Informs sparkle completions and AI gates; reports monthly budget |\n| `remote_runner_fleet` | Connected mTLS build runner nodes over port :8444 | Online runner count, heartbeat timestamp | Continuous | Distributed workload execution; heartbeats must arrive within 30s |\n| `rate_limiter` | In-memory token bucket and IP throttle health | Bucket capacity, blocked IP count, error rate | Continuous | Protects API endpoints from volumetric denial-of-service |\n| `realtime_pubsub_probe` | Active PostgreSQL LISTEN/NOTIFY latency measurement | Latency milliseconds (target < 1.0ms) | On Demand | Validates database pub/sub bus responsiveness |\n| `websocket_ws_probe` | Real WebSocket ping/pong frame roundtrip measurement | Latency milliseconds (target < 50ms) | On Demand | Validates client-to-server WebSocket bidirectional messaging |\n\n![Admin Diagnostics Realtime Probe Active](images/slice4-diagnostics-realtime-probe-active.png)\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs\n\n- **`postgresql_rls`:** Evaluates database health via `GET /api/v1/admin/diagnostics`. Asserts that the single-head Alembic migration tree is fully applied (82/82 migrations), connection pool saturation remains under 75%, and all 100 application tables enforce `FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY`. Connection latency is sampled continuously with healthy thresholds < 15ms.\n- **`realtime_hub_outbox`:** Tracks the transactional outbox relay daemon (`database/outbox/relay.py`). Measures the unprocessed message queue depth, average dispatch lag (healthy < 5s), and PostgreSQL `LISTEN/NOTIFY` channel subscription status. Stalled outboxes trigger immediate administrative alerts.\n- **`job_worker_pool`:** Monitors asynchronous background task executors responsible for webhook dispatch, automated snapshot creation, and audit logging. Reports total active tasks, concurrency utilization, and dead-letter queue (DLQ) depth. Any non-zero DLQ count indicates unhandled job failures requiring inspection.\n- **`wasm_script_sandbox`:** Monitors the in-process Wasmtime runtime executing custom field formulas and transition helpers. Verifies compiler initialization, memory consumption caps (default 32 MiB / 33,554,432 bytes per instance), and fuel budget limits preventing CPU exhaustion.\n- **`agent_runtime_providers`:** Asserts the availability and response latency of registered AI model providers (`gemini`, `openai`, `ollama`). Reports monthly token consumption against configured budget ceilings, warning administrators before cloud budget caps halt automated transition gates.\n- **`remote_runner_fleet`:** Tracks external execution nodes connected via mutual TLS (mTLS) over dedicated port `:8444`. Reports active runner counts, certificate expiration dates, and heartbeat timestamps. Runners failing to report within 30 seconds are marked offline and their active leases rescheduled.\n- **`rate_limiter`:** Reports the runtime state of in-memory token bucket rate limiters across public and authenticated routes. Tracks blocked request volumes and active IP throttle mappings, ensuring system resilience against abusive traffic.\n- **`realtime_pubsub_probe` and `websocket_ws_probe`:** On-demand latency probes. The pub/sub probe (`POST /api/v1/admin/diagnostics/probes/realtime`) injects a synthetic outbox notification and captures `LISTEN/NOTIFY` roundtrip time (typically ~0.38ms). The WebSocket probe initiates a live `/ws` binary ping frame in the operator's browser, measuring connection setup (~13.7ms) and ping/pong roundtrip latency (~0.44ms).\n\n---\n\n## 14. Universal Object Graph Link Auditor Studio\nThe Universal Object Graph Link Auditor Studio (`/admin/link-auditor`) inspects, classifies, and remediates referential integrity anomalies across all registered entities.\n\n![Link Auditor Studio - Desktop Light](images/slice4-link-auditor-1440x900-light.png)\n\n\n### Link Auditor Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `audit_ids` | Flagged link audit record UUIDs selected for purge | List of 1 to 200 UUIDs | Mandatory in purge | Scope of destructive link deletion in single atomic transaction |\n| `reason` | Mandatory administrative justification for link purge | String (10 to 500 characters) | Mandatory in purge | Recorded in immutable activity ledger; rejects < 10 chars with 422 |\n| `status_filter` | Filter tabs for inspecting audit record lifecycle | `unpurged`, `purged`, `resolved`, `all` | `unpurged` | Isolates active anomalies from resolved historical records |\n| `reason_filter` | Filters links by formal invalidity category | All 7 `InvalidLinkReason` codes | `ALL` | Isolates specific defect patterns for forensic inspection |\n\n![Link Auditor Integrity Scan Active](images/slice4-link-auditor-integrity-scan-active.png)\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs\n\n- **`audit_scan`:** Triggered via `POST /api/v1/admin/object-links:audit` (or the **Run Integrity Scan** button). Traverses `object_links` and returns summary counts across all 7 formal `InvalidLinkReason` codes without acquiring table locks.\n- **`reason_filter`:** Allows administrators to filter detected anomalies across the 7 formal `InvalidLinkReason` classes:\n  1. `DANGLING_SOURCE`: Source object UUID does not exist in the universal object spine.\n  2. `DANGLING_TARGET`: Target object UUID does not exist in the database.\n  3. `SOFT_DELETED_ENDPOINT`: One or both endpoints reference an object soft-deleted beyond the retention window.\n  4. `ARCHIVED_POLICY_VIOLATION`: Link connects active and archived entities violating project archiving rules.\n  5. `TYPE_MATRIX_VIOLATION`: Relation violates the registered link type matrix allowlist.\n  6. `CROSS_WORKSPACE_LEAK`: Link connects entities across disjoint workspace boundaries without federation.\n  7. `PERMISSION_SEVERED`: Endpoints span boundaries where caller or entity permissions have been severed.\n- **`audit_ids`:** The explicit list of 1 to 200 audit record UUIDs submitted to `POST /api/v1/admin/object-links:purge`. Submissions with fewer than 1 or more than 200 items are rejected with HTTP 422.\n- **`reason`:** The mandatory rationale string required by `POST /api/v1/admin/object-links:purge`. The string must contain at least 10 non-whitespace characters (e.g. `\"Decommissioning orphaned test links post-cutover\"`). Rationale strings containing fewer than 10 characters are rejected with HTTP 422.\n- **`status_filter`:** Selects the display subset in the Studio UI: `unpurged` (flagged items requiring operator intervention), `purged` (historically purged records), `resolved` (links where the underlying anomaly was repaired), or `all`.\n- **`immutable_audit_ledger`:** Every completed purge operation writes an immutable `INVALID_OBJECT_LINK_PURGED` event to `activity_records`. The response returns `purged_count` and the list of `activity_ids` emitted to the ledger for forensic verification.\n"
