- Zero OS syscall capability - Memory capped at 16 MB\"]\n    Dispatcher -->|Complex Logic / Scripts| TierB[\"Tier B: nsjail Container Jail - Linux namespaces (CLONE_NEWNET) - Read-only root filesystem - CPU timeout: 5 sec, Memory: 512 MB\"]\n    \n    TierA --> Verify[\"Verify Computed Value vs Pydantic Schema\"]\n    TierB --> Verify\n    Verify --> Store[\"Recorded in work item properties JSONB\"]\n```\n\n![Custom Fields on Ticket Properties Rail](images/36-rail-custom-fields.png)\n\n### Tier A: WebAssembly Runtime (Wasmtime)\n- Runs compiled WebAssembly modules in-process.\n- Provides sub-millisecond execution times.\n- Zero capability to open sockets, access the filesystem, or spawn processes.\n- Memory allocation strictly capped at 16 MB with instruction count fuel limits.\n\n### Tier B: Hardened Container Jail (nsjail)\n- Utilizes Linux kernel namespaces (`CLONE_NEWPID`, `CLONE_NEWNET`, `CLONE_NEWNS`).\n- Root filesystem mounted strictly read-only.\n- Network access completely disabled (`CLONE_NEWNET`).\n- Hard resource limits enforced via cgroups (512 MB RAM, 5-second wall clock timeout).\n\n---\n\n## Ephemeral Scoped Tokens and Tiered Privilege Escalation\n\nCustom scripts often need to query project metadata (such as counting open bugs or reading cycle end dates). To prevent privilege leakage:\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    autonumber\n    actor Script as Custom Scripted Field\n    participant Gate as Authorization Gate\n    participant Admin as Server/Workspace Admin (AAL2)\n    participant TokenService as Ephemeral Token Minter\n\n    Note over Script,TokenService: Default Execution: Project Scoped\n    Script->>Gate: Execute with default Project-scoped ephemeral token\n    \n    Note over Script,TokenService: Escalation Request: Workspace or Server\n    Script->>Gate: Request Escalated Scope (WORKSPACE or SERVER)\n    Gate->>Admin: Display Unmissable Blast Radius Warning Banner\n    Admin->>Gate: Cryptographic Signature with AAL2 Confirmation\n    Gate->>TokenService: Mint Time-Limited Ephemeral Escalated Token\n    TokenService-->>Script: Deliver Ephemeral Token for Execution\n```\n\n1. **Ephemeral Project Tokens:** By default, every script executes using an ephemeral API token scoped strictly to the current **Project**. It cannot read data from other projects or workspace settings.\n2. **Cryptographic Privilege Escalation:** If a script requires broader access (such as reading data across the entire **Workspace** or querying **Server** configuration), the script must request an escalated token scope. The UI displays an unmissable administrative warning banner detailing the blast radius, and the escalation must be signed with cryptographic approval by an administrator possessing authority over that target tier. Scripts can never escalate to Server Admin privileges without explicit multi-factor confirmation.\n\n![Custom Fields Dual Control Escalation Studio](images/slice5-custom-fields-escalation-dual-control-desktop-1440x900-light.png)\n![Custom Fields Dual Control Escalation Flow](images/52-custom-fields-dual-control.png)\n\n### Dual-Control Principle:\nTo satisfy enterprise SOC 2 and ISO 27001 separation of duties, escalating a custom scripted field to workspace-wide or server-wide scope requires dual-control authorization:\n- A project administrator can author and propose the escalation.\n- A distinct, independent administrator holding target tier authority must review the blast radius warning banner and supply fresh Authenticator Assurance Level 2 (AAL2) credentials to activate the escalated token scope.\n- All escalation grants are logged with SHA-256 cryptographic digests in the immutable audit ledger.\n"
